# <%= projectName %>

HTTP-first React SSR starter generated by `fluo new --starter react-vite-ssr`.

Requires Node.js `>=24.0.0 <27`. Use Node 24 LTS for local development, CI, and container images. The server build targets `node24` and uses `@types/node@^24.0.0`.

## First two pages

```sh
<%= installCommand %>
<%= devCommand %>
```

The generated `dev` script runs `fluo dev`, as does the installed `fluo` binary invoked
directly. After installation, neither path requires a manual production build: the CLI
loads the server entry through project-local Vite SSR transforms and serves client
modules and CSS through Vite middleware on the Fastify development server. Source
edits restart that server and are served from the changed source without creating
production `dist` or requiring a client build manifest. This is full-process reload,
not module-level HMR. `build` and `start` remain the separate production path.

Open `http://127.0.0.1:3000/products/sku-42?preview=true`, then follow
the Search catalog link to `/search?q=catalog`. Edit `src/page.tsx` and
`src/page-search.tsx`; their UI and hydrated interaction share the same server/client
document, provider, and page slot. The shell stays mounted during approved soft navigation;
page-local state resets when a new destination mounts. Before hydration, with JavaScript
disabled, on modified clicks, and on unavailable destinations, links remain native anchors.

The decorated route remains explicit in `src/app.ts`:

```ts
@Router('/products')
class ProductPageRouter {
  @Path('/:sku')
  @RequestDto(ProductPageRequest)
  show(input: ProductPageRequest) {
    const props = { sku: input.sku, preview: input.preview === 'true' };
    return ReactNavigationPage.create(createElement(ProductPage, props), {
      module: './page.tsx',
      props,
    });
  }
}
```

This handler still runs through `@fluojs/http` matching, DTO binding, validation, middleware,
guards, interceptors, request scopes, and not-found behavior before the application renderer sees
the returned page. The search handler likewise validates its `q` query DTO and selects
`./page-search.tsx` with one `query` prop. To add an ordinary third page, create
`src/page-about.tsx` with a default component accepting `Record<string, unknown>`,
then add its explicit `@Router`/`@Path` handler and DTO in `src/app.ts`; select
`ReactNavigationPage.create(createElement(AboutPage, props), {
module: './page-about.tsx', props })` and register that router in `ReactModule.forRoot`.
No renderer, client entry, manifest plumbing, or client matcher edits are needed: both entries
pick up `./page*.tsx` through Vite's build-produced importer map. The server checks that
the selected module is present before committing the document; the browser only imports
approved keys. Only JSON data belongs in `props`; never pass DI instances, server-only
modules, or secrets. Decorator-bearing application declarations stay in `.ts` files so the
Vite transform handles them; JSX rendering stays in `.tsx` modules such as `src/page.tsx`.

